00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.217
AHMET ALP BALKAN: So we
just finished our talk

00:00:02.217 --> 00:00:04.770
about Kubectl Plugins.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.350
As Google, we have developed
this Kubectl Plugin Manager,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.880
which is called Krew.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.010
In our talk, we
explained what are

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.140
some challenges in
the Kubectl Plugin

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.030
ecosystem, why should
you use plugins,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.738
and how would you
[INAUDIBLE] the plugin,

00:00:16.738 --> 00:00:19.490
how would you distribute it
and bring it to more users.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.430
So in our talk, we've gone
through some best practices.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.420
We explained how to
make plugins palatable

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.510
and what sort of problems
do plugins solve.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.610
So last time we talked,
Krew Plugin Manager

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.920
actually had something like 15
to 20 plugins that it hosted.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.010
But this year, we actually
have over 60 plugins.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.370
So the Kubectl Plugin
ecosystem is actually

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.580
growing pretty fast.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.510
So if you're a developer who's
interested in Kubectl Plugin

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.927
development, which is writing
your subcommands for Kubectl,

00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:47.010
please check out the talk.

